diff --git a/tools/jdk-determinism/winsweep/README.md b/tools/jdk-determinism/winsweep/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..5e55f4de
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/jdk-determinism/winsweep/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,74 @@
+# jdk-determinism/winsweep
+
+Windows counterpart of `../sweep.sh`: runs the agreed cross-runtime check set of the deterministic math under every JDK
+home given and prints one table, exit status 1 on any real difference. Batch `2026-09-27-jdk-determinism`, owner
+decision D8 (2026-09-28): "do GraalVM on win, rewrite tools if needed. Linux testing will be a separate work." `sweep.sh`
+(Linux, container JDKs from `fetch-jdks.sh`) is left unchanged; the Linux sweep of the final code is a later, separate
+piece of work. Written on branch `claude/determinism-rehearsal`; first run in
+`documentation/2026-09-27-jdk-determinism/INTEGRATION_REHEARSAL.md` section 14.
+
+## How to run (Git Bash, from any directory)
+
+```
+bash tools/jdk-determinism/winsweep/winsweep.sh <out-dir> <reference-jdk-home> <jdk-home>...
+# e.g. the five runtimes of this machine, HotSpot 21 as the reference:
+bash tools/jdk-determinism/winsweep/winsweep.sh build/winsweep/run1 \
+  "C:/Program Files/Java/jdk-21.0.11" "C:/Program Files/Zulu/zulu-25" \
+  "C:/Program Files/JetBrains/IntelliJ IDEA 2026.2.1/jbr" \
+  "D:/Minecraft/Modding/jdks/jdk-21.0.12.15" "D:/Minecraft/Modding/jdks/graalvm-community-25.4.4.1.1+1.1"
+```
+
+Env: `ROOT` (worktree under test, default the one holding this script), `SEEDS` (default `1-200`), `THREADS` (fuzz
+workers, default 4; the machine rule is at most 4 while another agent runs a campaign), `FUZZ=0` (skip the fuzz),
+`BUILD=auto|always|never`, `FUZZ_RUNNER` (the Gradle-free fuzz runner folder; default
+`<ROOT>/tools/phase-ports-probes/fuzz-runner`, else the `claude/fuzz-runner` worktree until that branch merges),
+`JAVAC_HOME` (compiles the two helper classes, default Oracle JDK 21.0.11).
+
+Output: `<out-dir>/table.md` (also printed), `details.txt` (every differing printed line, chain-100 line or fuzz seed),
+and per runtime `r<i>/`: `identity.txt` (`-XshowSettings:properties`, plus `UseJVMCICompiler`/`EnableJVMCI` from
+`-XX:+PrintFlagsFinal` on non-OpenJ9 VMs), `mathsweep.txt`, `suite.txt`, `regression.txt`, `fuzz-<dist>-report.txt`
+(+ `.classes.txt`, `.java.txt`), and on GraalVM `graal-compilation.log`.
+
+## What it does
+
+1. **Build, once, with Gradle** (only when missing or older than a file under `src/` or the probe sources): the fuzz
+   runner's `build.sh` compiles main and test classes and the probe sources, builds the mod jar, and writes the test
+   task's `java`, JVM arguments, class path and working directory into `<ROOT>/build/fuzz-runner/`. Then `javac`
+   compiles `../perf/src/MiniLauncher.java` (JUnit Platform launcher with package selectors) and `../src/MathSweep.java`
+   into `<ROOT>/build/winsweep/`. Everything after this is Gradle-free.
+2. **Per runtime** (the test task's JVM arguments and class path, only the `java` executable changes):
+   - identity and family: OpenJ9 by VM name, GraalVM by `UseJVMCICompiler = true`, otherwise HotSpot by major version;
+   - `MathSweep`: which `java.lang.Math` functions differ from `StrictMath` on this runtime and whether any is
+     tier-dependent (the libm the product must not depend on; nonzero on HotSpot and GraalVM, zero on OpenJ9);
+   - fluid suites: `package:science.fluid`, `package:runtime.fluid`, `runtime.Fluid*Test`, the probe classes of
+     `phase-ports-probes/hold-fuzz/src` and `hold-classes/src` excluded; on GraalVM with
+     `-Djdk.graal.PrintCompilation=true -Djdk.graal.LogFile=r<i>/graal-compilation.log`, so the table can show that the
+     Graal compiler compiled the science code and the `DeterministicMath`/`DeterministicPow` methods during the run;
+   - chain-100 exact (`FluidSolverRegressionTest`, `-Dfluid.regression.mode=exact`, never capture);
+   - placement-sequence fuzz, random and world-default distributions, `SEEDS`, `THREADS` workers, 120 slices.
+3. **Compare** (`compare.js`) against the first runtime:
+   - printed suite lines in order, after masking `ms=`, `allocatedMB=`, `bytes=`, `wall...=`; lines that report a wall
+     time (a number followed by `ms`, a `Nanos=` count) are ignored and counted; lines that differ only in the order of
+     a `reasons={...}` map (a `Map.copyOf`, randomised per JVM start; `INTEGRATION_REHEARSAL.md` decision 2) count as
+     identical; the set of failing tests must be the same;
+   - chain-100: the whole deviation line against the in-tree record and the step, solve, iteration and residual counts
+     must be the same text as the reference's;
+   - fuzz: per seed the first hold (class and full status text, or clean). A seed that differs with a budget hold
+     (class F: wall deadline / soft budget) on either side is "budget-only" (timing-dependent by design); any other
+     difference is real.
+
+## Adding a runtime
+
+Extract the JDK anywhere (not on PATH, no system setting) and pass its home directory; nothing else changes. A runtime
+must run class files of release 21 (the build's target) and accept the test task's JVM arguments (`-Xmx8g`, `-ea`,
+`-D...`, file encoding and locale; HotSpot 21/25, OpenJ9 21 and GraalVM 25 all do). Family detection covers HotSpot,
+OpenJ9 and GraalVM with the Graal JIT; a GraalVM run with `-XX:-UseJVMCICompiler` would be reported as HotSpot.
+
+## Cost in counted work (per runtime)
+
+- 5 JVM starts for checks (+ 2 short identity starts): `MathSweep` (11 functions x 1,048,576 inputs x 3 repetitions,
+  each through `Math` and `StrictMath`: about 69 million calls), the fluid suites (528 tests in the first run, 286
+  Newton solves in the mixed-gas cost case alone), chain-100 (1 test: 3 accepted steps, 4 Newton solves, 29 iterations),
+  and two fuzz runs of `SEEDS` seeds (default 2 x 200 seed runs of 120 five-second slices each, `THREADS` workers).
+- Build: one Gradle invocation when stale (none when the build is current) and two `javac` runs.
+- The fuzz reports print their own `seedMsSum`/`wallMs`; they are load-dependent and not a claim of this tool.
